external:        The public key infrastructure that issues the DoD-approved certificates,
                 and the certificate authority trust chain each receiver and sender
                 validates against, are managed outside the application.
external:        Forwarder-side settings in outputs.conf live on a separate host by
                 definition, so on an indexer only the receiving half of this criterion can
                 be read.
external:        Whether the address or name that connection_host records is the true
                 origin depends on the network path: an intervening relay, proxy or load
                 balancer substitutes its own address, and DNS must resolve consistently
                 for the "dns" setting to be meaningful.
external:        Mapping an authenticated source to an organization, system, application or
                 individual is an organizational naming and attribution scheme; the
                 application records the identifier it is given.

citation:        Three corrections. First, serverCert, sslPassword and requireClientCert
                 are moved to the [SSL] stanza. They are legal inside
                 [splunktcp-ssl:<port>] only as per-port deviations - "To specify global
                 TLS settings, that are applicable for all ports, add the settings to the
                 [SSL] stanza. Specify any TLS setting that deviates from the global
                 setting here" (inputs.conf.spec:919-932) - and the product STIG's own fix
                 for SPLK-CL-000290 writes [splunktcp-ssl:9997] with disabled = 0 and then
                 puts serverCert and sslPassword under [SSL]. Requiring them under the port
                 stanza therefore reports a finding against an instance built exactly as
                 the benchmark authority prescribes, and against every deployment that sets
                 TLS once for all ports; [SSL] is also the stanza whose values apply to all
                 receiving ports (inputs.conf.spec:986-1015). Second, inputs.conf host
                 non_empty is removed because it asks for the opposite of the requirement:
                 connection_host = none "leaves the host as specified in inputs.conf,
                 typically the hostname of the system running Splunk software"
                 (inputs.conf.spec:526-534), so a static host is how source identity is
                 lost, not retained, and requiring it alongside connection_host in ip|dns
                 demands both halves of a contradiction - it also carried an empty stanza
                 name and could not be evaluated. Third, the UDP rule was missing.
                 SPLK-CL-000270 shares this SRG and is checked as "If any input is
                 configured to use a UDP port, this is a finding" and fixed by replacing
                 UDP inputs with TCP ports; a datagram input authenticates nothing, so this
                 is the most direct application-layer condition the requirement has, and it
                 is machine-checkable against both UDP stanza forms (inputs.conf.spec:1227,
                 1314). requireClientCert true is kept deliberately: server-side TLS
                 authenticates the receiver, not the source, and the fix text's own
                 clientCert on the forwarder authenticates the sender only if the receiver
                 demands the certificate (inputs.conf.spec:1015-1023). connection_host
                 in:ip|dns is within the setting's vocabulary [ip|dns|none] and both
                 receiving defaults are already ip or dns (inputs.conf.spec:526-534,
                 889-891), so it fails only an explicit "none".
finding:         Splunk does not uniquely identify and authenticate the source of
                 transferred data: connection_host is set to "none" on a receiving input,
                 the receiver's TLS certificate or client-certificate requirement is unset
                 in inputs.conf [SSL], the forwarder presents no client certificate or does
                 not verify the receiver, Splunk Web is not served over HTTPS, or a UDP
                 input is configured, which the product STIG prohibits because it cannot
                 authenticate a sender.
